FINAL_ABSTRACT = """Urban mobility systems built on dense sensor deployments fail structurally rather than in isolation. A gateway goes offline and takes a dozen validators with it, a corridor closes and the assumed topology is no longer in force, and the sensors that drop out most often serve the districts least able to absorb discarded trips. Separate models for fault detection, repair, forecasting and revenue decisions each see one facet and none sees the interaction. This paper presents DyHGT-MARL, a dynamic heterogeneous graph Transformer coupled to a fairness constrained decision layer, with a reproducible synthetic benchmark that makes the interaction measurable. The benchmark harmonizes three previously separate synthetic files into 80,640 node-step records over 2,880 quarter-hour steps and 28 transit nodes, in a heterogeneous graph of 69 nodes and seven relation types. Cascades propagate over documented adjacency with temporal decay rather than over row order, and the corpus adds correlated gateway outages, peripheral dropout missing not at random, two corridor closures and a distribution shift. Across five seeds on chronological splits the model attains coverage of 0.952 against a nominal 0.95 and a calibration error of 0.023, and loses 4.2 percent of score at 40 percent missingness where a climatological baseline loses 95 percent. The decision layer raises net revenue recovery from 80.99 to 88.96 percent while closing a 0.152 equal opportunity gap between zones. Results that do not favour the system are reported in full. Gradient boosting and a multilayer perceptron detect faults far more accurately, calibration collapses under distribution shift, and the fairness constraint never binds. A linear policy trained by policy gradient reaches 93.80 percent revenue by accepting every record, using none of the abstention machinery, and the decision layer is a two-zone tabular contextual bandit rather than a multi-agent reinforcement learner. A replication cohort on a second parameterisation of the generator reproduces the robustness and fairness findings and overturns two others. Each outcome is traced to a property of the source generators."""


def main():
    t = SRC.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    for a, b in CUTS:
        if a not in t:
            sys.exit(f"TRIM3 anchor not found: {a[:70]}")
        t = t.replace(a, b, 1)
    applied = 0
    for a, b in PHRASES:
        if a in t:
            t = t.replace(a, b, 1)
            applied += 1
    print(f"phrase compressions applied: {applied} of {len(PHRASES)}")
    i = t.index("Urban mobility systems built on dense sensor deployments")
    j = t.index("\n\n**Keywords:**", i)
    t = t[:i] + FINAL_ABSTRACT + t[j:]
    SRC.write_text(t, encoding="utf-8")
    body = re.sub(r"\$\$.*?\$\$", " ",
                  re.sub(r"\[\[FIGURE:[^\]]+\]\]", "", t.split("## Author Contributions")[0]),
                  flags=re.S)
    print("body words:", len(re.sub(r"[#*`|>]", " ", body).split()))
    print("semicolons:", t.count(";"), " em dash:", t.count(chr(8212)))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
